"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: Can molecular mapping of AAV capsid-receptor interactions on brain microvascular endothelial cells determine which AAV variants transcytose across the blood-brain barrier versus remain intracellular, and for which capsid-receptor binding signatures?
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"Option 1: Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer)\",\"title\":\"Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer)\",\"summary\":\"Enumerate binding modes of engineered capsid libraries against the BBB receptor panel (LY6A, LY6C1, ALPL, LRP6, CA4, AAVR) with docking, MD refinement, and AlphaFold-Multimer to test whether transcytosis-competent pairs share a geometric motif such as 3-fold-axis engagement of GPI-anchored receptors (https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/POSTER-Voyager_ASGCT_2024_FINAL_Ishan.pdf). Published modeling covers only single-digit complexes, e.g. AlphaFold-Multimer models of LRP6 with AAV-X1 and CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptides (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518), against an experimental catalog of ~150 AAV capsid structures (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12682079).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on Q3 of the root question — for which capsid–receptor binding signatures transcytosis occurs — specifically whether a shared interface motif marks transcytosis-competent complexes.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute would buy model runs (capsid × receptor × pose); published measurements of its effect stop at single-digit numbers of AAV–receptor complexes modeled per study — two LRP6 peptide complexes in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518 — and above that it is unknown whether exhaustive enumeration would surface a shared transcytosis-competent motif or demonstrate that no such motif exists.\",\"sequential\":\"none: every capsid–receptor pair is an independent docking or AlphaFold-Multimer job with its own random seeds, so the work already splits into independent pieces.\"}
{\"id\":\"Option 2: Microsecond molecular dynamics of capsid–receptor–membrane assemblies\",\"title\":\"Microsecond molecular dynamics of capsid–receptor–membrane assemblies\",\"summary\":\"Run long MD of capsid plus receptor in a membrane at endosomal pH to watch binding poses, pH-triggered release, and raft/curvature effects: VCAP-102 binds ALPL at Kd ~20 nM at pH 7.4 but dissociates rapidly at pH 5.5 (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1.full.pdf), and coarse-grained Martini3 simulations of AAV2 with AAVR in a model membrane reached microsecond frames with PKD1/PKD2 contacts lasting only ~1 µs at a time (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11492248).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on Q1/Q3 — whether fate-relevant physics such as pH-dependent release and membrane-domain partitioning is already visible at the binding step, and so whether molecular mapping can be pushed toward a trafficking signature.\",\"compute_open\":\"settled: longer trajectories are already known to reveal more of the binding configurational space — the AAV2–AAVR coarse-grained study reports that once the capsid is membrane-bound its motion is restricted and much longer simulation times would be required to observe how the capsid moves on the membrane (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11492248).\",\"sequential\":\"One answer waits on a chain of ~2 fs integration steps (the timestep used in the AAV2–AAVR protocol, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11492248), each step consuming the previous step's forces and coordinates, so one 100 ns run is ~5×10^7 sequential steps and a 1 µs run ~5×10^8; the answer needs several independent replicas and a single replica keeps one GPU essentially fully busy (GPU offloading in gmx mdrun, https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/current/user-guide/mdrun-performance.html). The steps must run in order inside one trajectory, but replicas can run at once and recasting the answer as an ensemble of many short trajectories combined by Markov-state analysis would let them run simultaneously — a payoff only if the dynamics is metastable so short runs recombine — as tried for virus capsid assembly with many short unbiased trajectories run in parallel (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.1784).\"}
{\"id\":\"Option 3: Supervised sequence-to-transcytosis phenotype models\",\"title\":\"Supervised sequence-to-transcytosis phenotype models\",\"summary\":\"Train sequence-to-function models on paired (capsid features, transcytosis phenotype) data to rank variants for validation, following deep models that predicted AAV2 packaging viability from 201,426 assayed variants yielding 110,689 viable capsids (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41587-020-00793-4) and Fit4Function's six models trained on libraries of 240K variants or less that predicted macaque biodistribution (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11297966).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on Q2 — assigning each variant a fate — and on whether the binding-to-fate gap in Q1 can be closed statistically instead of mechanistically.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute would buy model runs (seeds, architectures) and model size; published measurements of its effect stop at ensembles of three architectures over ~2×10^5 labeled variants (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41587-020-00793-4) and six Fit4Function models over ≤240K-variant libraries (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11297966), and above that it is unknown whether more parameters, seeds, or training compute improve transcytosis prediction or whether labeled transcytosis data, not FLOPs, is the binding constraint.\",\"sequential\":\"One training run is a chain of roughly 10^4–10^5 gradient updates, each update waiting on the previous weights' loss, so a converged answer needs multiple independent seeds and one model per trait (Fit4Function trained six, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11297966), and a single run keeps one GPU busy throughout. Within a run the updates must run in order, but different seeds and per-trait models can train at the same time — which pays off when each model fits one GPU and the labeled set is fully visible to every run, the arrangement used by Fit4Function's RNN/LSTM sequence models (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11297966).\"}
{\"id\":\"Option 4: Cryo-EM reconstruction of capsid–receptor complexes\",\"title\":\"Cryo-EM reconstruction of capsid–receptor complexes\",\"summary\":\"Determine single-particle cryo-EM structures of capsid–receptor complexes, as for AAV2–AAVR (PKD2) at 2.4 Å (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6561701), to extend the ~150-structure AAV capsid library (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12682079); all high-resolution cryo-EM structures of capsid complexes are single-particle reconstructions averaging large numbers of homogeneous particles (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12682079).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the interface-mapping component of Q1 — which capsid residues and receptor domains contact — that feeds the binding signatures of Q3.\",\"compute_open\":\"settled: map resolution is already known to improve as more particles contribute — in the AAV structural atlas, genome-containing AAV7/11/12/13 reconstructions reached slightly lower resolution than the empty capsids specifically because fewer capsids were used in those reconstructions (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7828300).\",\"sequential\":\"One map waits on a chain of processing stages — motion correction, CTF estimation, particle picking, 2D classification, 3D refinement — roughly a dozen sequential stages each consuming the previous stage's particle stack; the answer needs one reconstruction per complex plus re-runs for empty/full or symmetry-broken states (the AAV2 Rep–capsid work produced separate pentameric and hexameric maps, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12713660), and a single refinement keeps one GPU busy for hours to days. The stages must run in order, but per-particle work inside a stage can run simultaneously — as in local refinements of 45,150 and 75,559 symmetry-expanded particles on GPU computing resources (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12713660) — which pays off when a dataset holds tens of thousands of independent particles.\"}
{\"id\":\"Option 5: Cross-species receptor–capsid compatibility scoring\",\"title\":\"Cross-species receptor–capsid compatibility scoring\",\"summary\":\"Score capsid interfaces against receptor orthologs (ALPL, LRP6, AAVR, and mouse-restricted LY6A/LY6C1) to predict which signatures translate to humans: AlphaFold modeling of VCAP-102–ALPL predicted capsid-contacting ALPL residues conserved across human, cynomolgus macaque, and mouse (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1.full.pdf), LRP6 emerged as a conserved BBB transcytosis receptor via interactome mapping plus AlphaFold-Multimer (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518), and LY6A/LY6C1 lack primate orthologs (https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pbio.3002112).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the species-conditionality caveat of Q3 — whether a binding signature learned in mouse survives in human.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute would buy model runs (receptor-ortholog × capsid pairs scored); published measurements of its effect stop at a handful of isoforms — ALPL contact residues compared for one capsid across human, cynomolgus macaque, and mouse (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1.full.pdf) — and above that it is unknown whether exhaustive ortholog scoring would keep, drop, or reorder the GPI-anchored-protein and LRP6 signatures as candidates for human BBB crossing.\",\"sequential\":\"none: each receptor-ortholog × capsid pair is an independent alignment and modeling job, so the work already splits into independent pieces.\"}

